CWPP MUSIC PROMPT — Yesterday-adjacent

Style & Era Constraints (high weight)
Mid-1960s British chamber-pop ballad aesthetic. Intimate, restrained, emotionally fragile. Feels like a personal confession recorded late at night in a quiet studio.

Tempo & Meter (high weight)
Slow tempo ~90–96 BPM, simple 4/4, no rhythmic drive — the song should feel like it rests rather than moves forward.

Harmony & Tonality (very high weight)
Center on F major–leaning tonality with frequent use of minor subdominant color (iv) and gentle descending bass motion.
Chords should feel nostalgic and unresolved — sadness implied through harmony, not performance drama.
Avoid blues language, avoid rock vamps, avoid modal grit.

Instrumentation (very high weight)
Solo acoustic guitar (nylon or warm steel), finger-picked, close-mic’d
String quartet (two violins, viola, cello) entering subtly, legato, supportive — never dominant
No drums
No bass guitar
No electric instruments
Strings should breathe with the vocal, entering and exiting like memory rather than arrangement.

Vocal Character (very high weight)
Single male vocal, soft, intimate, slightly nasal but warm.
Very close microphone proximity — breaths audible.
Delivery is resigned, reflective, almost apologetic.
No melisma. No belting. No vibrato flourishes.
Emotion comes from understatement, not power.

Melodic Shape (high weight)
Simple, singable melody with small intervals.
Melody often descends at phrase endings, reinforcing regret and reflection.
Avoid hooks that feel triumphant or catchy.

Production Constraints (high weight)
Mono or narrow stereo image
Analog warmth, tape saturation
Minimal reverb, short room feel
Performance imperfections preserved

Emotional Target (very high weight)
Bittersweet nostalgia.
Loss that is accepted, not fought.
A quiet realization that something good is gone and cannot be recovered.

Negative Constraints (hard blocks)
No drums or percussion
No modern pop production
No rhythmic groove
No vocal stacking or harmonies
No dramatic climax
No irony, humor, or theatricality